      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate for water meters.\n\nKey facts:\n- Certificate No: R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06, Revision 1\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Date issued: 9 January 2025 (signature date) — original issuing 11 November 2024\n- Applicant: JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A., Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288, 1950-421 Lisboa, PT\n- Manufacturer: same\n- Certified type: Water meter – ultrasonic, dry dial, Smartio\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013, accuracy class 2\n- Project number? The certificate number contains \"24.06\" — no explicit project number. There's \"V 3112\" at the top, that's a triangular emblem logo with \"V 3112\" — could be a form number. Not project number. There's evaluation report No. 0511-ER-V109-23. Project number not explicitly given; omit.\n- Page total: 7\n- OIML issuer ID: not given explicitly. Omit.\n\nType evaluation report: No. 0511-ER-V109-23 dated 11 November 2024, 25 pages including annex 1, role: type_evaluation_report\nTest report: No. 6015-PT-P5013-24, 201 pages including annex 1-2, role: test_report\nDocumentation file: 0511-UL-V109-23, role: documentation_file\n\nRevision history:\n- Revision \"-\": 11 November 2024, Issuing certificate\n- Revision 1: 9 January 2025, Correction of typo in company name\n\nCharacteristics:\nThe models vary by:\n- Nominal diameter: 15 or 20 (DN15, DN20)\n- Q3 values: for DN15: 1.6 or 2.5 m³/h; for DN20: 2.5 or 4.0 m³/h\n- R ratio Q3/Q1: 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125, 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500, 630, 800, 1000\n\nSo the model family is quite complex. Models are combinations of nominal diameter × Q3 × ratio R.\n\nLet me extract the flow tables carefully.\n\nTable 1 (DN15, R40-125):\nQ3/Q1: 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125 — each with two columns (Q3=1.600 first, Q3=2.500 second)\nQ1: 0.040, 0.063 | 0.032, 0.050 | 0.025, 0.040 | 0.020, 0.031 | 0.016, 0.025 | 0.013, 0.020\nQ2: 0.064, 0.100 | 0.051, 0.080 | 0.041, 0.064 | 0.032, 0.050 | 0.026, 0.040 | 0.021, 0.032\nQ3: 1.600, 2.500 (×6)\nQ4: 2.000, 3.125 (×6)\n\nTable 2 (DN15, R160-500):\nQ3/Q1: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500\nQ1: 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013 | 0.006, 0.010 | 0.005, 0.008 | 0.004, 0.006 | 0.003, 0.005\nQ2: 0.016, 0.025 | 0.013, 0.020 | 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013 | 0.006, 0.010 | 0.005, 0.008\nQ3: 1.600, 2.500 ×6\nQ4: 2.000, 3.125 ×6\n\nTable 3 (DN15, R630-1000):\nQ3/Q1: 630 (two columns: Q3=1.600 and Q3=2.500), 800 (one column, Q3=2.500), 1000 (one column, Q3=2.500)\nQ1: 0.0025, 0.004, 0.003, 0.0025\nQ2: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004\nQ3: 1.600, 2.500, 2.500, 2.500\nQ4: 2.000, 3.125, 3.125, 3.125\n\nTable 4 (DN20, R40-125):\nQ1: 0.063, 0.100 | 0.050, 0.080 | 0.040, 0.064 | 0.031, 0.050 | 0.025, 0.040 | 0.020, 0.032\nQ2: 0.100, 0.160 | 0.080, 0.128 | 0.064, 0.102 | 0.050, 0.080 | 0.040, 0.064 | 0.032, 0.051\nQ3: 2.500, 4.00 ×6\nQ4: 3.125, 5.00 ×6\n\nTable 5 (DN20, R160-500):\nQ1: 0.016, 0.025 | 0.013, 0.020 | 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013 | 0.006, 0.010 | 0.005, 0.008\nQ2: 0.025, 0.040 | 0.020, 0.032 | 0.016, 0.026 | 0.013, 0.020 | 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013\nQ3: 2.500, 4.00 ×6\nQ4: 3.125, 5.00 ×6\n\nTable 6 (DN20, R630-1000):\nQ1: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004\nQ2: 0.006, 0.010, 0.008, 0.006\nQ3: 2.500, 4.00, 4.00, 4.00\nQ4: 3.125, 5.00, 5.00, 5.00\nQ3/Q1: 630 (two columns: 2.5 & 4.0), 800 (4.0), 1000 (4.0)\n\nSo the model variants: DN15 and DN20, each with Q3 ∈ {1.6, 2.5} (DN15) or {2.5, 4.0} (DN20), and R = Q3/Q1 ∈ {40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125, 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500, 630, 800, 1000}.\n\nTotal combinations:\nDN15, Q3=1.6: R40-R630 (13 ratios: 40,50,63,80,100,125,160,200,250,315,400,500,630)\nDN15, Q3=2.5: R40-R1000 (15 ratios)\nDN20, Q3=2.5: R40-R630 (13 ratios)\nDN20, Q3=4.0: R40-R1000 (15 ratios)\n\nThat's 13+15+13+15 = 56 model variants.\n\nHow should I structure this? The models are defined by (nominal_diameter, q3, q3_over_q1). I'll create model IDs like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R=40\".\n\nModel family:\n- family_name: \"Smartio\"\n- models: list of 56 entries? That could be verbose but it's accurate. Alternatively I can define models as combinations of the two axes. Hmm.\n\nActually let me think about how to best present this. The model_level characteristics:\n- q1 varies by model (dn, q3, r)\n- q2 varies by model\n- q3 varies by model\n- q4 varies by model\n\nLet me construct all 56 models:\n\nDN15, Q3=1.6:\n- R40: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R50: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R63: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.041, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R80: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R100: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R125: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.021, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R160: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R200: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R250: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R315: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R400: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R500: Q1=0.003, Q2=0.005, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n- R630: Q1=0.0025, Q2=0.004, Q3=1.600, Q4=2.000\n\nDN15, Q3=2.5:\n- R40: Q1=0.063, Q2=0.100\n- R50: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080\n- R63: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064\n- R80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.050\n- R100: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040\n- R125: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032\n- R160: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025\n- R200: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020\n- R250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- R315: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013\n- R400: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010\n- R500: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008\n- R630: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006\n- R800: Q1=0.003, Q2=0.005\n- R1000: Q1=0.0025, Q2=0.004\nQ4 = 3.125 for all Q3=2.5.\n\nDN20, Q3=2.5:\n- R40: Q1=0.063, Q2=0.100\n- R50: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080\n- R63: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064\n- R80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.050\n- R100: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040\n- R125: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032\n- R160: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025\n- R200: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020\n- R250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- R315: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013\n- R400: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010\n- R500: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008\n- R630: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006\nQ4 = 3.125.\n\nDN20, Q3=4.0:\n- R40: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160\n- R50: Q1=0.080, Q2=0.128\n- R63: Q1=0.064, Q2=0.102\n- R80: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080\n- R100: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064\n- R125: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051\n- R160: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040\n- R200: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032\n- R250: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026\n- R315: Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020\n- R400: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- R500: Q1=0.008, Q2=0.013\n- R630: Q1=0.006, Q2=0.010\n- R800: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008\n- R1000: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.006\nQ4 = 5.00 for Q3=4.0.\n\nThis is a lot of data. 56 models × 4 attributes. That's the accurate representation but very verbose. Alternatively, I could use config_level with axes... The schema suggests config_level for configuration axes (attribute × config-axis matrix). Actually this fits: attributes vary by configuration of nominal_diameter, q3, and ratio.\n\nHmm. Which is better? The model_family approach with 56 models seems most faithful. Let me consider using model_level entries with values per model. That's 4 attributes × 56 values = 224 value objects plus 56 model entries. That's big but it's what the data says.\n\nAlternatively, I could structure model_level as config-level with the axis being \"nominal_diameter × Q3 × Q3/Q1 ratio\". Actually, per rule 5, config_level is for configuration axes. Here, the variants are really model variants (different flow configurations of the Smartio meter).\n\nGiven the schema example R137: explicit 2-D table → matrix_tables[] AND model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level. Here we have 6 tables. I'll create matrix_tables for each of the 6 flow tables (or combine). And populate model_family with models, and model_level characteristics.\n\nLet me decide on model_id format: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R40\" etc. Actually the photo shows \"DN25 Q3=6,3m3/h R=100\" — interesting, the photo shows a DN25 meter but the tables only cover DN15 and DN20. That's an example photo, ignore.\n\nWait — \"DN25\" in the photo... \"Q3=6,3m3/h R=100\" — but tables don't have DN25. The photograph is just an example. Stick with DN15/DN20 from tables.\n\nLet me generate model IDs compactly: \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R40\". \n\nFor the matrix_tables, I'll create one per table (6 tables) or combine into fewer. The schema has one \"name\" per table. I'll do 6 tables:\n\n1. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN15, R40–125\"\n2. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN15, R160–500\"\n3. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN15, R630–1000\"\n4. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN20, R40–125\"\n5. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN20, R160–500\"\n6. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) – DN20, R630–1000\"\n\nEach with columns: q3_over_q1_ratio, q1, q2, q3, q4, nominal_diameter? Or per the layout: rows are attributes and columns are variants. Hmm, in the actual tables, rows are Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4/ratio and columns are the variants. For matrix_tables I should transpose into rows-per-model format.\n\nRows: {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"q3_over_q1\": 40, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q1\": 0.040, \"q2\": 0.064, \"q4\": 2.0}\n\nThat's cleaner. So each matrix table has rows for each variant.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: one combined matrix_table with all 56 rows? But the schema allows multiple tables. I'll do 6 to preserve fidelity to the document, or... hmm, 6 tables with column_units. Let me do it.\n\nActually, to reduce redundancy, maybe I should combine per nominal diameter into 2 tables (DN15 with 28 rows, DN20 with 28 rows)? But the R630/R800/R1000 columns differ in structure. The rows format handles it fine: DN15 R630 appears with Q3=1.6 and Q3=2.5 as two rows; R800 and R1000 with Q3=2.5.\n\nI think 6 matrix_tables matching the document tables is most faithful. Let me go with that.\n\nNow for model_family.models: 56 entries with model_id, label, attributes (nominal_diameter, q3, ratio). Let me include attributes in each model entry: {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"q3\": 1.6, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 40}.\n\nAnd characteristics.model_level: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4 with values per model. That's 4 × 56 = 224 entries. Verbose but correct. Hmm, that's a huge JSON. Alternatively use config_level for q1/q2 as derived from ratio... \n\nLet me think about what's most useful. The schema says model_level is \"attribute × model matrix\". With 56 models this becomes enormous. But rules say to extract. I'll do it — accuracy over brevity. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe I can make the model_id encode the parameters and then model_level only includes q1, q2, q3, q4.\n\nModel ID: \"Smartio DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\"? Or shorter \"DN15-1.6-R40\". I'll use \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R40\".\n\nLet me now enumerate all models systematically.\n\nDN15, Q3=1.6 (13 models):\nR40: q1=0.040, q2=0.064, q4=2.000\nR50: q1=0.032, q2=0.051\nR63: q1=0.025, q2=0.041\nR80: q1=0.020, q2=0.032\nR100: q1=0.016, q2=0.026\nR125: q1=0.013, q2=0.021\nR160: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\nR200: q1=0.008, q2=0.013\nR250: q1=0.006, q2=0.010\nR315: q1=0.005, q2=0.008\nR400: q1=0.004, q2=0.006\nR500: q1=0.003, q2=0.005\nR630: q1=0.0025, q2=0.004\n\nDN15, Q3=2.5 (15 models):\nR40: q1=0.063, q2=0.100\nR50: q1=0.050, q2=0.080\nR63: q1=0.040, q2=0.064\nR80: q1=0.031, q2=0.050\nR100: q1=0.025, q2=0.040\nR125: q1=0.020, q2=0.032\nR160: q1=0.016, q2=0.025\nR200: q1=0.013, q2=0.020\nR250: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\nR315: q1=0.008, q2=0.013\nR400: q1=0.006, q2=0.010\nR500: q1=0.005, q2=0.008\nR630: q1=0.004, q2=0.006\nR800: q1=0.003, q2=0.005\nR1000: q1=0.0025, q2=0.004\nq4=3.125 for all.\n\nDN20, Q3=2.5 (13 models):\nR40: q1=0.063, q2=0.100\nR50: q1=0.050, q2=0.080\nR63: q1=0.040, q2=0.064\nR80: q1=0.031, q2=0.050\nR100: q1=0.025, q2=0.040\nR125: q1=0.020, q2=0.032\nR160: q1=0.016, q2=0.025\nR200: q1=0.013, q2=0.020\nR250: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\nR315: q1=0.008, q2=0.013\nR400: q1=0.006, q2=0.010\nR500: q1=0.005, q2=0.008\nR630: q1=0.004, q2=0.006\nq4=3.125.\n\nDN20, Q3=4.0 (15 models):\nR40: q1=0.100, q2=0.160\nR50: q1=0.080, q2=0.128\nR63: q1=0.064, q2=0.102\nR80: q1=0.050, q2=0.080\nR100: q1=0.040, q2=0.064\nR125: q1=0.032, q2=0.051\nR160: q1=0.025, q2=0.040\nR200: q1=0.020, q2=0.032\nR250: q1=0.016, q2=0.026\nR315: q1=0.013, q2=0.020\nR400: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\nR500: q1=0.008, q2=0.013\nR630: q1=0.006, q2=0.010\nR800: q1=0.005, q2=0.008\nR1000: q1=0.004, q2=0.006\nq4=5.0.\n\nTotal: 13+15+13+15 = 56 models.\n\nNow type_level characteristics (whole-cert attributes):\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_zone: ±5% → value -5? or \"±5\"? Hmm. MPE as ±5% — value could be {min: -5, max: 5}? Simpler: value: 5, unit: \"%\", attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrate_zone\" with value \"±5\"? Rules say numbers not strings. I could express as {min: -5, max: 5}. That seems reasonable. Or value: 5 with attribute noting it's a limit. I'll use {min: -5, max: 5} unit \"%\" for both MPEs.\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- pressure_class: \"MAP16\" (maximum admissible pressure MAP 16)\n- pressure_loss_class: varies by model! Q3 1.6 → Δp10, Q3 2.5 → Δp16 (DN15), Q3 2.5 → Δp16, Q3 4.0 → Δp25 (DN20). Actually look: DN15 column: \"Q3 1.6 ∆p10, Q3 2.5 ∆p16\"; DN20 column: \"Q3 2.5 ∆p16, Q3 4.0 ∆p25\". So pressure loss class is config-level varying by Q3: 1.6→10, 2.5→16, 4.0→25 (Δp class in kPa presumably). This is config_level with axis \"Q3\".\n  Actually the marking section says \"The pressure loss class (∆p 10, ∆p 16, ∆p 25)\" — consistent.\n  So config_level: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"nominal_flowrate_q3\", values: [{condition: \"Q3=1.6 m³/h\", value: 10}, {condition: \"Q3=2.5 m³/h\", value: 16}, {condition: \"Q3=4.0 m³/h\", value: 25}]. Unit: kPa (Δp class number is in kPa per R49... actually Δp classes in R49 are 10, 16, 25, 40, 63, 100 kPa). Unit_symbol \"kPa\"? The document doesn't state unit. Hmm, the marking says \"∆p 10\" without unit. R49 defines pressure-loss classes Δp in kPa. I'll leave unit null to avoid fabrication... Actually it's standard knowledge that ∆p classes are in kPa, but document doesn't say. Rule 8: don't fabricate. I'll set unit_symbol null and keep value as the class number, or treat value as string \"∆p10\". I'll use value 10 etc. with unit null. Hmm, but is the class a number? \"∆p10\" - the class designation. I'll put value as string \"Δp10\"? The rules prefer numbers... The class is effectively a numeric class. I'll use numbers with unit_symbol \"kPa\" — risky. Let me keep value numeric, unit null. Actually let me just be safe: value: 10, unit_symbol: null, and the attribute name pressure_loss_class. The axis conditions carry Q3.\n\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50, unit \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit \"MPa\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999, unit \"m³\"; user mode: 999999 m³. The cell: \"9 999 / 999 999\" for testing mode/user mode. Split into two attributes: indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999 m³; indicating_range_user_mode: 999999 m³.\n- resolution_indicating_device_testing_mode: 0.00001 m³; user mode: 0.001 m³\n- resolution_device_rapid_testing: 100 pulse/dm³\n- resolution_indicating_device_rapid_testing: 0.000001 m³\n- eut_category: \"Ultrasonic water meters, Coriolis water meters, fluidic water meters\"\n- eut_case: \"B\"\n- connection_type: DN15: \"NPSM or G type ¾\", 7/8\", 1\"\"; DN20: \"NPSM or G type 7/8\", 1\", 1 1/4\"\" — config level by nominal diameter. Hmm. Or model level. It varies by nominal diameter (column). The two columns in the Characteristics table correspond to DN15 and DN20. I'll add config_level: attribute \"connection_type\", axis \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"NPSM or G type 3/4\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\"}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"NPSM or G type 7/8\\\", 1\\\", 1 1/4\\\"\"}].\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: false (\"No\")\n- mounting: \"-\" → omit or \"not specified\". Rule 8: omit.\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- length: ≥105 mm (both columns). value {min: 105, max: null} unit mm. Note the cell says \"≥ 105mm\" and header says [mm].\n- reverse_flow: \"Designed to measure\"\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O\" — class B or O. Value as string \"B, O\"? Marking section says \"Environmental classification (B or O)\". I'll put value \"B or O\" or \"B, O\". Keep \"B, O\".\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 70} °C\n- power_supply_type: \"Non replaceable battery\" (battery). Also text says \"powered mains battery 3.6V\" — \"mains battery\" is odd phrasing; power supply table says \"Non replaceable battery\".\n- u_max: 3.6 V\n- u_min: 1.9 V\n- power_supply_frequency: \"-\" → omit (footnote 1 marker... the footnote 1 text is about Q3/Q1 ratio, seems misplaced, but footnote markers exist on the Power supply rows).\n- minimum_battery_lifetime: 16 years\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q3_over_q4: 1.25\n- volume_resolution_display: 0.00001 m³ (from description)\n- software versions: \"0.6.28\", \"1.0.1\" — two SW versions, each with CRCs: 0x854EBACF, 0x13372073 (for 0.6.28?) and 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40. Actually 4 CRCs for 2 versions. Hmm: \"CRC checksum (of legally relevant SW): 0x854EBACF, 0x13372073 / 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40\". Probably SW 0.6.28 has CRCs 0x854EBACF, 0x13372073 and SW 1.0.1 has 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40. I'll record as list of strings.\n  - software_version: [\"0.6.28\", \"1.0.1\"] — value as list? The structured value shape doesn't include lists. I can put value: \"0.6.28, 1.0.1\" as string. Or use model-level? No. I'll use value as array — schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. A list isn't allowed. String \"0.6.28; 1.0.1\". I'll do that. Similarly CRCs as one string.\n\nFootnote 1: \"The ratio Q3 / Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\" — appears on power supply rows. Extract into footnotes[] with marker \"1)\". Also \"≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5\" has a \"1\" superscript? \"≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5m3/h\" — hmm, that \"1\" might be a footnote marker (1) meaning ratio per footnote. Actually looking: \"Q3/Q1: ≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5m3/h, ≤ 630 1 for Q3=1.6m3/h | ≤ 1000 for Q3=4m3/h, ≤ 630 for Q3=2,5m3/h\". The \"1\" after 1000 and 630 in the first column is likely footnote marker ¹. So ratio Q3/Q1 max values have footnote marker \"1)\".\n\nInteresting: the Q3/Q1 row in the characteristics table: for DN15: ≤1000 for Q3=2.5, ≤630 for Q3=1.6; for DN20: ≤1000 for Q3=4.0, ≤630 for Q3=2.5. Wait that says for DN20, Q3=2.5 the ratio is limited to ≤630? But the flow table 4 (DN20) includes R40...R630 for Q3=2.5 — yes consistent (13 ratios max 630). And DN20 Q3=4.0 up to 1000 — consistent. DN15: Q3=1.6 up to 630, Q3=2.5 up to 1000 — consistent with tables.\n\nSo config_level for \"maximum_ratio_q3_q1\": axis Q3:\n- Q3=1.6: {max: 630}\n- Q3=2.5: {max: 1000}\n- Q3=4.0: {max: 1000}\nwith footnote marker \"1)\".\n\nNow the ratio values themselves are per model — included in model attributes.\n\nWhere does q1/q2/q3/q4 go? model_level. OK.\n\nAlso marking information — should I extract as characteristics? Not really characteristics; skip mostly. The description field of certified_type can hold description.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"Smartio\"]\n- module_designation: \"-\" → null. The designation of module row shows \"-\", meaning not applicable. Per schema, \"Not applicable\" or null. I'll use null.\n- description: brief.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.06\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present. Omit? There's \"V 3112\" top-right — that's likely a form/design number of CMI. I'll omit.\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: Original 11 November 2024; revision 1 dated 9 January 2025 (signature date). Which to use? date_issued — the certificate was issued 11 Nov 2024, revision 1 on 9 Jan 2025. The printed date at signature: 9 January 2025. Hmm. The revision history shows \"-\" 11 November 2024 \"Issuing certificate\". I'll set date_issued: \"2024-11-11\" and revision history covers the rest. Or \"2025-01-09\"? The signature date is the current (revision 1) issue date. I think date_issued: \"2024-11-11\" (original issuing). Hmm, but the document as revised is dated 9 January 2025. I'll go with 2025-01-09 as the document date? Let me think: \"Date: 9 January 2025\" at signature. Revision 1 date: 9 January 2025. The original: 11 November 2024. I'll use date_issued \"2024-11-11\" since that's when the certificate was issued, and revision_history documents the revision. Either defensible. I'll go with \"2024-11-11\".\n\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present; omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Czech Metrology Institute\"\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" (on page 1) — also \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\" signed. person_responsible per doc: Jan Kalandra. The signing person: Pavel Klenovský with title Head of Certification Body. I'll put person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\", person_title: null... Hmm, the schema has person_responsible and person_title. Page 1: \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". Page 2 signature: \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and add person_title? Title for Kalandra unknown. Alternatively person_responsible could be the signer. I'll keep Jan Kalandra (explicitly labeled \"Person responsible\") and omit title. \n- website: not present; omit. Actually CMI website... not in doc, omit.\n\napplicants[]: JANZ - Contagem e Gestao de Fluídos, S.A., address: [\"Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288\", \"1950-421 Lisboa\", \"Portugal\"]? The doc says \"PT\". Keep \"PT\" as given: [\"Avenida Infante D. Henrique, 288\", \"1950-421 Lisboa\", \"PT\"].\n\nmanufacturers[]: same.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null/omit\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [2] — as number? Example shows accuracy_classes[] list; use [2] or [\"2\"]. I'll use [2]... The