o:80, q3:1.6, q1:0.020, q2:0.032, q4:2.0}\n- {dn:15, ratio:80, q3:2.5, q1:0.031, q2:0.050, q4:3.125}\n- {dn:15, ratio:100, q3:1.6, q1:0.016, q2:0.026, q4:2.0}\n- {dn:15, ratio:100, q3:2.5, q1:0.025, q2:0.040, q4:3.125}\n- {dn:15, ratio:125, q3:1.6, q1:0.013, q2:0.021, q4:2.0}\n- {dn:15, ratio:125, q3:2.5, q1:0.020, q2:0.032, q4:3.125}\n\nTable B (DN15, R160–500):\n- 160: 0.010/0.016, 0.016/0.025\n- 200: 0.008/0.013, 0.013/0.020\n- 250: 0.006/0.010, 0.010/0.016\n- 315: 0.005/0.008, 0.008/0.013\n- 400: 0.004/0.006, 0.006/0.010\n- 500: 0.003/0.005, 0.005/0.008\n\nTable C (DN15, R630–1000):\n- 630 Q3=1.6: q1 0.0025, q2 0.004, q4 2.0\n- 630 Q3=2.5: q1 0.004, q2 0.006, q4 3.125\n- 800 Q3=2.5: q1 0.003, q2 0.005, q4 3.125\n- 1000 Q3=2.5: q1 0.0025, q2 0.004, q4 3.125\n\nTable D (DN20, R40–125):\n- 40: Q3=2.5: 0.063/0.100; Q3=4.0: 0.100/0.160\n- 50: 0.050/0.080; 0.080/0.128\n- 63: 0.040/0.064; 0.064/0.102\n- 80: 0.031/0.050; 0.050/0.080\n- 100: 0.025/0.040; 0.040/0.064\n- 125: 0.020/0.032; 0.032/0.051\n\nTable E (DN20, R160–500):\n- 160: 0.016/0.025; 0.025/0.040\n- 200: 0.013/0.020; 0.020/0.032\n- 250: 0.010/0.016; 0.016/0.026\n- 315: 0.008/0.013; 0.013/0.020\n- 400: 0.006/0.010; 0.010/0.016\n- 500: 0.005/0.008; 0.008/0.013\n\nTable F (DN20, R630–1000):\n- 630 Q3=2.5: 0.004/0.006\n- 630 Q3=4.0: 0.006/0.010\n- 800 Q3=4.0: 0.005/0.008\n- 1000 Q3=4.0: 0.004/0.006\n\nWait, check Table 3 (DN15 R630-1000) again:\nQ1: 0.0025, 0.004, 0.003, 0.0025\nQ2: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004\nQ3: 1.600, 2.500, 2.500, 2.500\nQ4: 2.000, 3.125, 3.125, 3.125\nRatios: 630 (2 cols), 800, 1000. ✓.\n\nTable 6 (DN20): Q1: 0.004, 0.006, 0.005, 0.004; Q2: 0.006, 0.010, 0.008, 0.006; Q3: 2.500, 4.00, 4.00, 4.00; Q4: 3.125, 5.00×3. ✓.\n\nNow should I include q3 and q4 in model_level? That adds 112 more value objects. Total model_level values = 56×4 = 224. It's fine, JSON can handle it. But response length... Each value object ~ {\"model\": \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R40\", \"value\": 0.04} ≈ 45 chars. 224 × 45 ≈ 10KB plus models list 56 × ~110 chars ≈ 6KB, plus matrix tables 56 rows × ~110 ≈ 6KB. Total maybe 30-40KB. Acceptable.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: to keep it manageable, maybe I should skip separate q3/q4 model_level entries since they're in model attributes and matrix tables. Hmm, but schema example included them. I'll include q1 and q2 in model_level (the truly derived varying ones), and q3/q4 also. Let me just include all four — completeness.\n\nActually wait — q3 and q4 ARE in the model attributes already (I planned attributes {nominal_diameter, q3, q3_over_q1}). q4 not in attributes. Let me include q4 in attributes too: {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q3_over_q1\": 40}. Then model_level only needs q1, q2. Hmm, but the R137 example put qmax/qt/qmin in model_level. To follow the pattern, I'll put q1, q2, q3, q4 in model_level and keep model attributes minimal (nominal_diameter, q3, q3_over_q1). q4 derivable. OK final decision: model_level has q1, q2, q3, q4.\n\nHmm, this is getting really long. Let me reconsider — maybe trim to q1 and q2 in model_level, since q3 is in attributes and q4 = q3 × 1.25 constant ratio. Eh. The safest complete approach: all four. I'll do all four. Actually, let me reconsider length constraints — the output will be long but that's fine.\n\nAlternatively, I can reduce by expressing q1 as a config-level attribute with axis ratio... no, it depends on 3 axes. Model_level is right.\n\nLet me also double check the \"Q3/Q1 ≤ 1000 1\" footnote markers — I'll add footnote_markers [\"1)\"] on the config-level max ratio values and on... the q3_over_q1 attribute in model entries? Probably fine to put on config_level max values only.\n\nNow other type-level characteristics:\n\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50, unit \"°C\" (also matches T50)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 1.6 MPa, class \"MAP16\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit \"MPa\"\n- pressure_loss_class config level.\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP 16\"\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: ±5% → {min:-5,max:5} %\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone: ±2% → {min:-2,max:2} %\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q3_over_q4: 1.25\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999 m³\n- indicating_range_user_mode: 999999 m³\n- resolution_indicating_device_testing_mode: 0.00001 m³\n- resolution_indicating_device_user_mode: 0.001 m³\n- resolution_rapid_testing_device: 100 pulse/dm³ — unit \"pulse/dm³\"\n- resolution_indicating_device_rapid_testing: 0.000001 m³\n- eut_test_category: \"Ultrasonic water meters, Coriolis water meters, fluidic water meters\"\n- eut_case: \"B\"\n- connection_type: config level by DN.\n- min straight lengths: 0 mm each\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\" (marking) — same as flow profile sensitivity class; one entry enough (flow_profile_sensitivity_class U0D0).\n- flow_conditioner_required: false\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- length: {min:105, max:null} mm\n- reverse_flow_measurement: \"designed to measure\" — value \"Designed to measure\"\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O\"\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:70} °C\n- power_supply_type: \"Non replaceable battery\"\n- battery_voltage_max: 3.6 V (u_max)\n- battery_voltage_min: 1.9 V\n- minimum_battery_lifetime: 16 years (unit \"years\")\n- software_version: \"0.6.28; 1.0.1\" (string; multiple versions)\n- crc_checksum: \"0x854EBACF, 0x13372073, 0x3A681C19, 0x1d901C40\"\n- software type: \"embedded software type P\", extensions II (water meters), S (separation), D (download). Maybe software_type: \"P (embedded, built-for-purpose)\" and extensions. I'll add: software_type: \"P\"; software_extensions: \"II (Water meters), S (Software separation), D (Download of Legally Relevant Software)\". As strings.\n- volume_resolution_display: 0.00001 m³ (from description) — same as